//@version=6

// ============================================================================
// Initial Balance [IB Lab.] (NY + London sessions)
// ============================================================================
// Draws the Initial Balance (IB) — the first hour of a session — for the NY
// and London sessions, using fixed New York and London session definitions.
// Works on any chart symbol with intraday data; built and tuned on CME index
// and commodity futures (NQ, ES, GC).
// Session and break definitions mirror the reference implementation in
// pipeline/build_data.js (~124-186) and
// lib/ib-core/analyze.ts (~72-79); keep this port in sync with those files.
//
// SESSIONS
// --------
// NY:      IB window 09:30-10:30 America/New_York (RTH open, first hour).
//          Session end 16:00 America/New_York (RTH close).
//
// London:  IB window 08:00-09:00 Europe/London (real UK wall clock — DST is
//          handled by TradingView's own Europe/London timezone support, not
//          manual offset arithmetic).
//          Session end 08:30 America/New_York, EXCLUSIVE (last included bar
//          is 08:29 ET). This is a cross-timezone window: post-IB session
//          membership is the INTERSECTION of a broad London-anchored window
//          ("0800-1330" Europe/London — 13:30 UK is the widest the session
//          can ever reach, per the pipeline/build_data.js comment) and a
//          NY-anchored cap ("0000-0830" America/New_York). During the ~2-3
//          weeks/year when the US and UK DST transitions don't align, the
//          intersection naturally ends earlier in UK wall-clock terms than
//          13:30 — that is correct and matches the reference implementation.
//
// Both session strings are weekday-only (":23456" = Mon-Fri).
//
// BREAK RULE (lib/ib-core/analyze.ts:72-79)
// ------------------------------------------
// After the IB window ends, the FIRST bar whose WICK exceeds an IB level
// breaks it: high > ibHigh breaks the high side, low < ibLow breaks the low
// side (strict inequality). The broken side's line ends ON the break bar and
// is never extended again. An unbroken line extends to the last bar of the
// session and stops there.
//
// Break detection runs on whatever timeframe the chart is on: it is an EXACT
// match with the reference implementation on the 5-minute timeframe (its
// canonical analysis timeframe) and only an APPROXIMATION on any other
// intraday timeframe (a different bar size can see a different
// first-touching bar/wick).
//
// IB MIDPOINT
// -----------
// (IB high + IB low) / 2, dashed. It cannot be "broken" — once the IB window
// closes it is fixed and always extends to the end of the session, whatever
// happens to the high/low lines. Drawn only while "Retracement from" is Off:
// with retracement on, the solid -0.5 rung is the only 50% line (see LEVELS).
//
// LEVELS (retracement + extension, lib/ib-core/analyze.ts + stats.ts)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Retracement is created ONCE per day, at the moment the IB window closes
// (never during the developing IB), anchored on the "Retracement from" input
// ("IB start" | "IB end" | "Off"). Its 9 solid lines (own color input,
// default black — distinct from the session colors so they stand out) sit
// INSIDE the IB at 0.1..0.9 of the IB range (0.5 included), labeled as a
// SIGNED scale: 0 sits at the bias TARGET edge. The bias is the IB drift
// direction, matching the reference implementation's Break Bias tile (lib/ib-core/render/day.ts:
// low printed first -> "Upside (L->H)" -> bias long, 0 at ibHigh; high
// printed first -> "Downside (H->L)" -> bias short, 0 at ibLow; a same-bar
// tie resolves to short, same rule as the break tie below). The rungs count
// NEGATIVE into the IB: -0.1 next to the 0 edge through -0.9 next to the
// opposite edge. While
// "Retracement from" is not "Off" the dashed IB midpoint is suppressed
// entirely (including its developing phase) — the solid -0.5 rung is the
// only 50% line; with "Off" the midpoint behaves exactly like v1, gated by
// its own "IB midpoint" toggle.
//
// Break vs bias: a first break WITH the bias changes nothing — 0 already
// sits at the broken edge, so the extension pair simply appears. A first
// break AGAINST the bias inverts the whole scale in place: 0 moves to the
// actually broken edge and every retracement LABEL is re-texted (rung k
// becomes -(1 - 0.1k)) — the lines themselves never move, since the same 9
// rungs sit at identical physical prices whichever edge they are measured
// from. Either way the day ends with 0 at the broken edge and the
// retracement counting negative into the IB — the reference implementation's max-retracement
// convention, just signed (owner decision 2026-07-24). A same-bar break tie
// resolves to bear, matching analyzeDay()'s `firstUp` tie-break; a later,
// second-side break on a double-break day changes nothing further
// (first-break direction only).
// A zero-range IB (ibHigh == ibLow) creates no levels at all.
//
// Extension: a signed +/- pair, 4 dotted lines each (own retracement color,
// dotted, fully opaque), created ONCE at the day's first break and anchored
// there — the "+" ladder runs +0.25..+1.00 outward from the BROKEN edge in
// the break direction, the "-" ladder runs -0.25..-1.00 outward from the
// OPPOSITE edge the other way. This mirrors the reference implementation's "beyond 0.25"
// threshold card (EXT_REACH_MIN in lib/ib-core/stats.ts) with a symmetric
// cap. A later, second-side break on a double-break day creates nothing.
//
// Both retracement and extension are unbreakable targets: once created they
// extend bar by bar to the end of the session exactly like the midpoint,
// never stopped by a later touch.
//
// Rolling window: level lines/labels (not the base IB lines/box/midpoint,
// which keep v1's TradingView-managed-cap behavior) are kept for the last N
// session-days per session (default 20, "Levels: last N days") and deleted
// exactly, oldest day first, once that window is exceeded. Worst case per
// session-day: 9 retracement + 4+4 extension = 17 lines and 17 labels — with
// both sessions and all elements on that is ~38 lines/day against
// TradingView's hard 500-per-type drawing caps (the declared maximums), so
// only roughly the last ~13 days can show COMPLETE levels; beyond that
// TradingView silently removes the oldest drawings, old days' base IB lines
// included (operations on such removed objects are no-ops, so the exact
// window deletion stays safe). Lower N to trade recent levels for deeper
// base-IB history; "all days" is impossible by platform design.
// ============================================================================

indicator("Initial Balance [IB Lab.]", shorttitle = "IB [IB Lab.]",
     overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500)

// ---- session strings (IANA timezones, "HHMM-HHMM:days", 2-6 = Mon-Fri) ----
NY_TZ               = "America/New_York"
NY_IB_SESSION        = "0930-1030:23456"   // IB hour: 09:30-10:30 ET
NY_FULL_SESSION      = "0930-1600:23456"   // full RTH session: 09:30-16:00 ET

LONDON_TZ             = "Europe/London"
LONDON_IB_SESSION     = "0800-0900:23456"  // IB hour: 08:00-09:00 UK wall clock
LONDON_BROAD_SESSION  = "0800-1330:23456"  // widest possible UK-time reach of the post-IB window
LONDON_NY_CAP_SESSION = "0000-0830:23456"  // hard NY-time cap: session ends 08:30 ET exclusive

// ---- inputs ----
GRP_NY   = "NY session"
GRP_LDN  = "London session"
GRP_DISP = "Display"
GRP_LVL  = "Levels"

showNy   = input.bool(true, "Enable NY IB", group = GRP_NY)
colorNy  = input.color(#e0a534, "NY color", group = GRP_NY)

showLdn  = input.bool(true, "Enable London IB", group = GRP_LDN)
colorLdn = input.color(#4ea8de, "London color", group = GRP_LDN)

showDevIB = input.bool(true, "Developing IB", group = GRP_DISP,
     tooltip = "When off, lines/box/midpoint only appear once the IB window has ended.")
showBox   = input.bool(true, "IB box", group = GRP_DISP)
showMid   = input.bool(true, "IB midpoint", group = GRP_DISP,
     tooltip = "Applies only while Retracement from (Levels group) is Off — the solid -0.5 retracement rung replaces the dashed midpoint otherwise.")
lineWidth = input.int(2, "Line width", minval = 1, maxval = 10, group = GRP_DISP)

retrMode        = input.string("IB start", "Retracement from", options = ["IB start", "IB end", "Off"], group = GRP_LVL)
retrColor       = input.color(color.black, "Retracement color", group = GRP_LVL,
     tooltip = "Solid, fully opaque — pick a light color on dark chart themes.")
showExt         = input.bool(true, "Extension levels", group = GRP_LVL)
showLevelLabels = input.bool(true, "Level labels", group = GRP_LVL)
levelDays       = input.int(20, "Levels: last N days", minval = 1, maxval = 60, group = GRP_LVL,
     tooltip = "TradingView hard-caps every indicator at 500 lines and 500 labels. With both sessions and all elements on (~38 lines/day) only roughly the last ~13 days can show complete levels — TradingView silently drops the oldest drawings beyond that, old base IB lines included. Lower N to keep deeper base-IB history instead.")

// ---- derived level toggles (computed once, passed down to f_updateSession) ----
retrOn        = retrMode != "Off"        // retracement rungs active (extensions have their own independent toggle)
retrFromStart = retrMode == "IB start"   // false => anchor retracement lines at IB end instead
midEnabled    = showMid and not retrOn   // dashed midpoint only while retracement is fully Off

// ---- level ladder constants (IB-range units) ----
RETR_STEP    = 0.1   // retracement step: 0.1 .. 0.9 of the IB range, including 0.5
RETR_COUNT   = 9
EXT_STEP     = 0.25   // extension step, matches EXT_REACH_MIN in lib/ib-core/stats.ts
EXT_MAX      = 1.0    // extension cap: signed +/- pair, 4 rungs each side
EXT_COUNT    = int(math.round(EXT_MAX / EXT_STEP))   // 4 rungs per ladder at the current EXT_STEP/EXT_MAX

// ---- per-session drawing state (mutated in place; UDTs are reference types) ----
type SessionState
    float ibHigh     = na
    float ibLow      = na
    int   ibStartBar = na
    int   ibEndBar   = na
    int   ibhBar     = na   // bar_index that FIRST set today's final IB high (strict >, mirrors ibhT)
    int   iblBar     = na   // bar_index that FIRST set today's final IB low (strict <, mirrors iblT)
    bool  brokeHigh  = false
    bool  brokeLow   = false
    line  hiLine     = na
    line  loLine     = na
    line  midLine    = na
    box   ibBox      = na
    // ---- levels (retracement + extension) — see the LEVELS header section ----
    bool  firstBreakSet     = false   // has today's first break happened yet?
    bool  firstBreakBull    = false   // true = high broke first (extension pair anchored from ibHigh outward)
    bool  retrAnchorBull    = false   // edge today's retracement labels are measured from (true = ibHigh)
    array<line>  levelLines        = na   // all live level lines in the rolling window, oldest-first
    array<label> levelLabels       = na   // all live level labels in the rolling window, oldest-first
    array<int>   dayLineCounts     = na   // per-day line counts, oldest-first, parallel with levelLines
    array<int>   dayLabelCounts    = na   // per-day label counts, oldest-first, parallel with levelLabels
    array<line>  activeLevelLines  = na   // today's level lines only — cheap per-bar extend loop
    array<label> activeLevelLabels = na   // today's level labels only — cheap per-bar move loop
    array<label> todayRetrLabels   = na   // today's retracement labels, creation order — aliases of levelLabels objects

var SessionState nyState  = SessionState.new()
var SessionState ldnState = SessionState.new()

// ---- timeframe guard: intraday, <= 60 min, and divides 60 evenly ----
tfSeconds = timeframe.in_seconds()
isValidTf = timeframe.isintraday and tfSeconds % 60 == 0 and tfSeconds <= 3600 and 3600 % tfSeconds == 0

var table noteTable = table.new(position.bottom_right, 1, 1)
if not isValidTf and barstate.islast
    table.cell(noteTable, 0, 0, "Use an intraday timeframe that divides evenly into 60 min",
         text_color = color.gray, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.small)

// ---- level helpers (retracement + extension ladders, rolling last-N-days window) ----
// Every helper below is written with single-branch `if`s (no `else`) only —
// see the CE10235 comment further down for why: any `if/else` whose branches'
// last statements have different types fails to compile, so new code sticks
// to single-branch `if`s whose (discarded) value can never conflict with
// anything, plus `while`/`for..in` loops, which are not typed expressions.

// bumps the count for "today" — always the LAST element of a per-day count array
f_incrLastCount(counts) =>
    if not na(counts) and array.size(counts) > 0
        int idx = array.size(counts) - 1
        array.set(counts, idx, array.get(counts, idx) + 1)

// creates one ladder of `count` level lines, anchored at `anchorBar`, each
// `stepSize * k` IB-range units from `edgePrice` in the `dirSign` direction.
// Pushes every created line/label into both the rolling (all live days) and
// active (today only) arrays and bumps today's object counts. `prefix` is
// prepended to the label's numeric text ("" for retracement, "+"/"-" for the
// signed extension pair).
f_createLevels(s, anchorBar, edgePrice, rangeVal, dirSign, stepSize, count, lineCol, lineStyle, lblCol, showLbl, prefix) =>
    for k = 1 to count
        float lvl = edgePrice + dirSign * k * stepSize * rangeVal
        line ln = line.new(x1 = anchorBar, y1 = lvl, x2 = anchorBar, y2 = lvl, xloc = xloc.bar_index,
             extend = extend.none, color = lineCol, style = lineStyle, width = 1)
        array.push(s.levelLines, ln)
        array.push(s.activeLevelLines, ln)
        f_incrLastCount(s.dayLineCounts)
        if showLbl
            // "0.##" (not "#.##"): the leading-zero digit is mandatory in
            // DecimalFormat semantics, so 0.3 renders "0.3", never ".3"
            string txt = prefix + str.tostring(k * stepSize, "0.##")
            label lbl = label.new(x = anchorBar, y = lvl, text = txt, xloc = xloc.bar_index,
                 color = color.new(lblCol, 100), style = label.style_label_left, textcolor = lblCol, size = size.small)
            array.push(s.levelLabels, lbl)
            array.push(s.activeLevelLabels, lbl)
            f_incrLastCount(s.dayLabelCounts)

// extends every level line/label created so far TODAY to the current bar —
// the same "unbreakable target" behavior as the IB midpoint, just applied to
// the whole active-day list instead of a single line
f_extendActiveLevels(lineArr, labelArr) =>
    if not na(lineArr)
        for ln in lineArr
            if not na(ln)
                line.set_x2(ln, bar_index)
    if not na(labelArr)
        for lbl in labelArr
            if not na(lbl)
                label.set_x(lbl, bar_index)

// rolling last-N-days window: while more than `maxDays` day slots are being
// tracked, pops the oldest slot's exact object count off the front of the
// count arrays and deletes exactly that many objects off the front of the
// rolling line/label arrays (both arrays are oldest-first and grow in lockstep
// with the count arrays, so this is always an exact, ordered deletion)
f_pruneOldestDay(s, maxDays) =>
    // one loop drops a day slot from BOTH count arrays atomically — they are
    // always pushed together at isNewIbBar, and a single loop keeps that
    // invariant explicit instead of relying on two while conditions staying
    // in sync
    while array.size(s.dayLineCounts) > maxDays
        int n = array.shift(s.dayLineCounts)
        int remaining = n
        while remaining > 0
            line ln = array.shift(s.levelLines)
            if not na(ln)
                line.delete(ln)
            remaining := remaining - 1
        int m = array.shift(s.dayLabelCounts)
        int remainingLbl = m
        while remainingLbl > 0
            label lbl = array.shift(s.levelLabels)
            if not na(lbl)
                label.delete(lbl)
            remainingLbl := remainingLbl - 1

// ---- shared per-session update logic (written once, called per session) ----
// ibActive/isNewIbBar/ibJustEnded/dayStart are derived once per session at
// the top level (they need history-referencing on a plain series, not a
// function parameter) and passed in as plain booleans.
f_updateSession(s, ibActive, isNewIbBar, ibJustEnded, fullActive, dayStart, col, showDev, showBx, showMd, width,
     retrOn, retrFromStart, retrCol, showXt, showLbl, maxDays) =>
    // one-time lazy init: UDT array-field defaults must be `na` (a UDT field's
    // default value cannot call array.new()) — guard-initialize here instead;
    // this only ever runs once per session, on that session's very first bar
    if na(s.levelLines)
        s.levelLines := array.new<line>()
    if na(s.levelLabels)
        s.levelLabels := array.new<label>()
    if na(s.dayLineCounts)
        s.dayLineCounts := array.new<int>()
    if na(s.dayLabelCounts)
        s.dayLabelCounts := array.new<int>()
    if na(s.activeLevelLines)
        s.activeLevelLines := array.new<line>()
    if na(s.activeLevelLabels)
        s.activeLevelLabels := array.new<label>()
    if na(s.todayRetrLabels)
        s.todayRetrLabels := array.new<label>()

    if dayStart and not isNewIbBar
        // the session started today but the IB window itself produced zero
        // bars (holiday/short/gapped session) — forget any stale state left
        // over from a previous day so nothing carries into today; previously
        // drawn objects on the chart are left untouched
        s.ibEndBar := na
        s.ibHigh := na
        s.ibLow := na
        s.ibStartBar := na
        s.ibhBar := na
        s.iblBar := na
        s.hiLine := na
        s.loLine := na
        s.midLine := na
        s.ibBox := na
        s.brokeHigh := false
        s.brokeLow := false
        s.firstBreakSet := false
        s.firstBreakBull := false
        s.retrAnchorBull := false
        s.activeLevelLines := array.new<line>()
        s.activeLevelLabels := array.new<label>()
        s.todayRetrLabels := array.new<label>()

    if ibActive
        // ---- developing IB: accumulate the running high/low, plus the bar
        // that FIRST set each (drives the retracement bias — whichever
        // extreme printed first). Strict-improvement single-branch ifs only,
        // no else: mirrors ibhT/iblT's strict >/< in pipeline/build_data.js. ----
        if isNewIbBar
            s.ibHigh := high
            s.ibLow := low
            s.ibhBar := bar_index
            s.iblBar := bar_index
            s.ibStartBar := bar_index
            s.brokeHigh := false
            s.brokeLow := false
        if high > s.ibHigh
            s.ibHigh := high
            s.ibhBar := bar_index
        if low < s.ibLow
            s.ibLow := low
            s.iblBar := bar_index

        if isNewIbBar
            // new IB day: reset first-break tracking, open a fresh active-level
            // list, and slide the rolling last-N-days window (push today's slot,
            // then prune the oldest slot if the window is now over maxDays)
            s.firstBreakSet := false
            s.firstBreakBull := false
            s.activeLevelLines := array.new<line>()
            s.activeLevelLabels := array.new<label>()
            s.todayRetrLabels := array.new<label>()
            array.push(s.dayLineCounts, 0)
            array.push(s.dayLabelCounts, 0)
            f_pruneOldestDay(s, maxDays)

        s.ibEndBar := bar_index
        float mid = (s.ibHigh + s.ibLow) / 2

        if showDev
            // Creation and update are deliberately NOT an if/else: Pine treats
            // if/else as an expression and rejects branches whose last
            // statements have different types (here `:= box.new` vs a void
            // set_* call, error CE10235). Creating in a single-branch if and
            // then updating unconditionally keeps every branch void — on the
            // creation bar the update just re-sets the same values.
            if isNewIbBar
                s.hiLine := line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = s.ibHigh, x2 = bar_index, y2 = s.ibHigh,
                     xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_solid, width = width)
                s.loLine := line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = s.ibLow, x2 = bar_index, y2 = s.ibLow,
                     xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_solid, width = width)
                if showMd
                    s.midLine := line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = mid, x2 = bar_index, y2 = mid,
                         xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_dashed, width = 1)
                if showBx
                    s.ibBox := box.new(left = s.ibStartBar, top = s.ibHigh, right = bar_index, bottom = s.ibLow,
                         border_color = color.new(col, 50), border_width = 1, bgcolor = color.new(col, 90), xloc = xloc.bar_index)
            if not na(s.hiLine)
                line.set_y1(s.hiLine, s.ibHigh)
                line.set_y2(s.hiLine, s.ibHigh)
                line.set_x2(s.hiLine, bar_index)
            if not na(s.loLine)
                line.set_y1(s.loLine, s.ibLow)
                line.set_y2(s.loLine, s.ibLow)
                line.set_x2(s.loLine, bar_index)
            if showMd and not na(s.midLine)
                line.set_y1(s.midLine, mid)
                line.set_y2(s.midLine, mid)
                line.set_x2(s.midLine, bar_index)
            if showBx and not na(s.ibBox)
                box.set_top(s.ibBox, s.ibHigh)
                box.set_bottom(s.ibBox, s.ibLow)
                box.set_right(s.ibBox, bar_index)
    else
        // ---- post-IB: create the frozen objects if "Developing IB" was off, then anchor retracement, then break-track / extend ----
        if ibJustEnded and not showDev and not na(s.ibEndBar)
            float mid = (s.ibHigh + s.ibLow) / 2
            s.hiLine := line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = s.ibHigh, x2 = s.ibEndBar, y2 = s.ibHigh,
                 xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_solid, width = width)
            s.loLine := line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = s.ibLow, x2 = s.ibEndBar, y2 = s.ibLow,
                 xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_solid, width = width)
            s.midLine := showMd ? line.new(x1 = s.ibStartBar, y1 = mid, x2 = s.ibEndBar, y2 = mid,
                 xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.none, color = col, style = line.style_dashed, width = 1) : na
            s.ibBox := showBx ? box.new(left = s.ibStartBar, top = s.ibHigh, right = s.ibEndBar, bottom = s.ibLow,
                 border_color = color.new(col, 50), border_width = 1, bgcolor = color.new(col, 90), xloc = xloc.bar_index) : na

        // ---- LEVELS: retracement is created ONCE, right here at IB close,
        // anchored on the bias (whichever IB extreme printed first — see the
        // LEVELS header section). Deliberately its own sibling `if`, NOT
        // nested inside the "not showDev" block above: retracement creation
        // must not depend on that toggle. A zero-range IB creates nothing. ----
        if ibJustEnded and retrOn and not na(s.ibEndBar)
            float ibRangeRetr    = s.ibHigh - s.ibLow
            bool  validRangeRetr = not na(ibRangeRetr) and ibRangeRetr != 0
            if validRangeRetr
                // bias = the IB drift direction, exactly the reference implementation's
                // Break Bias tile (lib/ib-core/render/day.ts:136 — loFirst -> "Upside
                // (L->H)", hiFirst -> "Downside (H->L)"): the extreme printed
                // LAST is the expected break target and carries 0. Low printed
                // first -> bias long, 0 at ibHigh; high first -> bias short,
                // 0 at ibLow; a same-bar tie resolves to short, matching the
                // break tie rule below.
                bool biasBull = s.iblBar < s.ibhBar
                s.retrAnchorBull := biasBull
                int retrAnchorBar = retrFromStart ? s.ibStartBar : s.ibEndBar
                f_createLevels(s, retrAnchorBar, biasBull ? s.ibHigh : s.ibLow, ibRangeRetr,
                     biasBull ? -1 : 1, RETR_STEP, RETR_COUNT, retrCol, line.style_solid, retrCol, showLbl, "-")
                s.todayRetrLabels := array.copy(s.activeLevelLabels)

        if fullActive and not na(s.ibEndBar)
            // wick-break check: strict inequality, first post-IB bar to exceed a level breaks it
            bool justBrokeHigh = false
            bool justBrokeLow  = false
            if not s.brokeHigh
                if not na(s.hiLine)
                    line.set_x2(s.hiLine, bar_index)
                if high > s.ibHigh
                    s.brokeHigh := true
                    justBrokeHigh := true
            if not s.brokeLow
                if not na(s.loLine)
                    line.set_x2(s.loLine, bar_index)
                if low < s.ibLow
                    s.brokeLow := true
                    justBrokeLow := true
            // midpoint: unbreakable, always extends to the last bar of the session
            if showMd and not na(s.midLine)
                line.set_x2(s.midLine, bar_index)

            // ---- LEVELS: the day's first break re-texts the bias-anchored
            // retracement labels if the realized break direction differs from
            // the bias they were created with (lines stay put — the 9 rungs
            // sit at the same physical prices from either edge), then creates
            // the signed +/- extension pair anchored on the break bar; a
            // zero-range IB creates nothing, and a later, second-side break on
            // a double-break day changes nothing further (first-break
            // direction only) ----
            float ibRange    = s.ibHigh - s.ibLow
            bool  validRange = not na(ibRange) and ibRange != 0
            if validRange and not s.firstBreakSet and (justBrokeHigh or justBrokeLow)
                s.firstBreakSet := true
                // same-bar tie (both sides break together) resolves to bear, matching analyzeDay()'s firstUp tie-break
                bool firstBreakBull = justBrokeHigh and not justBrokeLow
                s.firstBreakBull := firstBreakBull
                // a break WITH the bias changes nothing (0 already sits at the
                // broken edge); a break AGAINST the bias inverts the signed
                // scale in place — 0 moves to the actually broken edge, rung
                // k's label becomes -(1 - 0.1k). See the LEVELS header.
                // the size guard is load-bearing, not defensive style: Pine's
                // `for 0 to -1` iterates DOWNWARD (implicit -1 step), so an
                // empty array (labels off, retracement Off, chart starting
                // mid-day) would hit array.get(_, 0) and die at runtime.
                // s.todayRetrLabels is never na here (lazy-initialized at the
                // top of this function).
                if firstBreakBull != s.retrAnchorBull and array.size(s.todayRetrLabels) > 0
                    for i = 0 to array.size(s.todayRetrLabels) - 1
                        label lbl = array.get(s.todayRetrLabels, i)
                        if not na(lbl)
                            label.set_text(lbl, "-" + str.tostring(1 - (i + 1) * RETR_STEP, "0.##"))
                    s.retrAnchorBull := firstBreakBull
                if showXt
                    f_createLevels(s, bar_index, firstBreakBull ? s.ibHigh : s.ibLow, ibRange,
                         firstBreakBull ? 1 : -1, EXT_STEP, EXT_COUNT, retrCol, line.style_dotted, retrCol, showLbl, "+")
                    f_createLevels(s, bar_index, firstBreakBull ? s.ibLow : s.ibHigh, ibRange,
                         firstBreakBull ? -1 : 1, EXT_STEP, EXT_COUNT, retrCol, line.style_dotted, retrCol, showLbl, "-")

            f_extendActiveLevels(s.activeLevelLines, s.activeLevelLabels)

// ---- NY session membership ----
nyIbTime      = time(timeframe.period, NY_IB_SESSION, NY_TZ)
nyIbActive    = not na(nyIbTime)
nyIsNewIbBar  = nyIbActive and na(nyIbTime[1])
nyIbJustEnded = na(nyIbTime) and not na(nyIbTime[1])
nyFullTime    = time(timeframe.period, NY_FULL_SESSION, NY_TZ)
nyFullActive  = not na(nyFullTime)
nyDayStart    = nyFullActive and na(nyFullTime[1])   // first bar of today's full session, IB or not

// ---- London session membership ----
// IB window: real UK wall clock, DST handled by the Europe/London tz.
londonIbTime      = time(timeframe.period, LONDON_IB_SESSION, LONDON_TZ)
londonIbActive    = not na(londonIbTime)
londonIsNewIbBar  = londonIbActive and na(londonIbTime[1])
londonIbJustEnded = na(londonIbTime) and not na(londonIbTime[1])
// post-IB membership = intersection of the broad UK-anchored window and the NY-anchored 08:30-ET-exclusive cap
londonBroadActive = not na(time(timeframe.period, LONDON_BROAD_SESSION, LONDON_TZ))
londonNyCapActive = not na(time(timeframe.period, LONDON_NY_CAP_SESSION, NY_TZ))
londonFullActive  = londonBroadActive and londonNyCapActive
// londonFullActive is a derived bool (not a raw time() na/non-na value), so
// route it through a float na-proxy to get the same unambiguous na()-based
// day-start idiom used for NY above (avoids negating a possibly-na bool[1]).
londonFullVal     = londonFullActive ? 1.0 : na
londonDayStart    = londonFullActive and na(londonFullVal[1])

// ---- run (timeframe guard applies to all drawing side effects) ----
if isValidTf and showNy
    f_updateSession(nyState, nyIbActive, nyIsNewIbBar, nyIbJustEnded, nyFullActive, nyDayStart,
         colorNy, showDevIB, showBox, midEnabled, lineWidth, retrOn, retrFromStart, retrColor, showExt, showLevelLabels, levelDays)
if isValidTf and showLdn
    f_updateSession(ldnState, londonIbActive, londonIsNewIbBar, londonIbJustEnded, londonFullActive, londonDayStart,
         colorLdn, showDevIB, showBox, midEnabled, lineWidth, retrOn, retrFromStart, retrColor, showExt, showLevelLabels, levelDays)